	(text "The car's own 12 V outlet (not part of the build)" (exclude_from_sim no) (at 34.29 278.384 0) (effects (font (size 1.6 1.6)) (justify left bottom)) (uuid "80277a7d-038f-545f-ae1e-44e17c453d9b"))
	(text_box "PROPOSED WIRING PRACTICES (verify ratings before implementation)\n-  Red = +12 V and black = earth, everywhere.\n-  One wire per crimp barrel, with the barrel matched to the wire size. No doubled wires.\n-  Spade connectors only on flat tabs; a ring terminal on the 12 V socket's centre stud.\n-  A rubber boot over the back of the 12 V socket, so the bare stud can never touch the seat-belt buckle.\n-  Sleeving and clips on every run, and grommets wherever a cable passes through the board.\n-  Keep every cable away from the seat rails, the seat-belt buckle, the gear lever and the EPB / drive-mode / EV switches.\n-  Mount the third LED head (H3) properly or remove it.\n-  No car-body earth and no fuse-box tap, unless I confirm that is how it was originally powered.\n-  Optional: if the new button has a 12 V LED ring, wire the ring from the switched side to earth.\n\nPOWER BUDGET at 12 V: USB up to ~4.5 A, Qi pad ~1.5-2 A, lights ~0.5-1 A = about 7 A before anything is plugged into the 12 V socket. These are estimates, not measured currents. Verify the combined load against the outlet, wiring and connector ratings; the fuse is not a load regulator." (exclude_from_sim no) (at 128.27 200.66 0) (size 113.03 82.55) (margins 1.5 1.5 1.5 1.5) (stroke (width 0.2) (type solid)) (fill (type color) (color 245 248 255 1)) (effects (font (size 1.6 1.6)) (justify left top)) (uuid "583e4b0f-ac92-5b1c-af83-fcfd558f0056"))
	(text_box "ABOUT THIS SHEET\nI designed the panel and planned its circuit before fabrication. This present-day drawing proposes a revised circuit; it is not the installed wiring and has not been built or validated. For the installed arrangement, see console_as_built.pdf. Ratings are recommendations; wire sizes and values are approximate.\nFrom the original build: the only control is a silver push button that turns on the RGB LEDs. The red-ringed part is a 12 V socket, not a switch." (exclude_from_sim no) (at 243.84 200.66 0) (size 162.56 22.86) (margins 1.5 1.5 1.5 1.5) (stroke (width 0.2) (type solid) (color 170 0 0 1)) (fill (type color) (color 255 240 240 1)) (effects (font (size 1.7 1.7)) (justify left top)) (uuid "c72fb30f-6b92-5f1c-9baf-c0d13bd92c82"))
	(text_box "STILL TO CHECK before building this version\n-  The car's outlet: that it is off when the car is off, and its rating (usually 120 W / 10 A on a ~15 A fuse; check the owner's manual).\n-  U1 and the Qi module must accept up to ~14.6 V (the original controller's label says 5-12 V).\n-  The original button latches; its current rating is unknown. Specify a switch rated 2 A DC or more." (exclude_from_sim no) (at 243.84 226.06 0) (size 162.56 20.32) (margins 1.5 1.5 1.5 1.5) (stroke (width 0.2) (type solid)) (fill (type color) (color 255 255 255 1)) (effects (font (size 1.55 1.55)) (justify left top)) (uuid "d29bdbb0-5715-5ea3-a9a1-5bea6aa1c935"))
	(sheet_instances (path "/" (page "1")))
	(embedded_fonts no)
)
